WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
PRESENTER: We covered monitoring
GKE infrastructure and services

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.530
in the previous episode.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:07.080
But GKE is not the only
place you can run containers

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.100
in Google Cloud.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.010
Today, we'll look at three
options for running containers,

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.440
and how to instrument each
one for observability,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.880
with Cloud Operations.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.000
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.600
Let's start by
reviewing your options

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.520
for running containers
in Google Cloud

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.310
and how you might
choose between them.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.190
Your first option is, of course,
GKE, or Google Kubernetes

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.060
Engine.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.640
It's a great way to
deploy, scale, and manage

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.430
Kubernetes clusters in the Cloud
and use them to orchestrate

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.150
your container workloads.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.780
GKE is the industry's first
fully managed Kubernetes

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.810
service, and it implements
the full Kubernetes API,

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.240
four-way autoscaling,
release channels,

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.460
and multicluster support.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.850
Your second option is Cloud
Run, either as a fully managed

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.850
serverless offering
or on GKE clusters.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.340
Cloud Run abstracts away
infrastructure management

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.890
by automatically scaling
up and down from zero,

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.489
and almost instantaneously by
responding to incoming traffic.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.550
And Cloud Run only charges
you for the exact resources

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:08.960
you use.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.850
Your third option is Anthos.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.650
It's a managed
application platform

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.230
that extends Google Cloud
Services and engineering

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.860
practices to your environment
so you can modernize apps

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:24.150
faster and establish operational
consistency across them.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.940
With so many options,
how do you choose?

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.360
GKE is a fantastic option if
you prefer to manage and operate

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.780
Kubernetes clusters directly,
or need that level of control

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.400
over your infrastructure.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.160
GKE is also great because of
its Google Cloud marketplace

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.080
integration, which
lets you access

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.760
enterprise-ready
containerized solutions

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.340
with pre-built deployment
templates, portability,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.190
simplified licensing,
and consolidated billing.

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.400
Cloud Run is a great choice as
a fully serverless platform.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.490
It makes app development and
deployment simple and fast,

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.700
and it's fully integrated with
Cloud Code, Cloud Build, Cloud

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.580
monitoring, and Cloud Logging
for a complete end-to-end

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.300
developer experience.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.410
Finally, Anthos is the right
choice for many enterprises

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.810
where hybrid or
multi-cloud is a reality.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.699
It lets you extend the
best of Google Cloud

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:12.520
to your environments, no
matter where they are, so you

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.340
can modernize apps and establish
operational consistency

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.470
across infrastructure.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.120
Now that you understand
your options,

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.040
let's have a look at how
to instrument your services

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.450
running on them
to send telemetry

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.790
to Cloud Operations for
a consistent approach

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.140
to observability.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.700
Let's start with GKE.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.773
We've covered much of
this in previous episodes,

00:02:30.773 --> 00:02:32.690
but let's have a look
at how to get visibility

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:37.057
into both the infrastructure
and your services.

00:02:37.057 --> 00:02:38.640
Let's start by
inspecting our cluster.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.040
Here, we can confirm that we
have both system and workload

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.190
logging enabled, and monitoring
enabled for the cluster.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.970
This means that we'll get logs
from our containers and cluster

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.870
infrastructure, and
infrastructure metrics

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.740
ingested automatically.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.680
Let's head over to
monitoring and have a look.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.570
We'll start at
the GKE dashboard.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.760
Here, we can see information
about our clusters,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.830
their nodes, and the
workloads and services

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:18.390
running on them, even down to
the pod and container level.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.830
We can select a
specific workload

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.330
to get information about it.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:25.850
Here, we can see if it's
generating any alerts, metrics

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.860
that show its
health and behavior,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.590
and the logs it's generating.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.440
In this case, the
workload is a deployment.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.690
If we want to know what pods
or containers are running

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.480
in this deployment, we can
filter the entire dashboard

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.070
by that resource.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.770
We can then see the actual
container running our workload,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.855
and get its metrics and
even container logs.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.380
Note that workloads
on GKE do not

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.472
have automatic tracing enabled.

00:03:55.472 --> 00:03:56.930
Have a look at our
previous episode

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:58.850
on distributed tracing
with OpenTelemetry

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.570
if this is something you need.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:04.180
GKE is well integrated
with Cloud Operations,

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:06.190
and makes it easy to
monitor and observe

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:08.560
both your infrastructure
and services.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.590
Now, let's have a look at
managing services on Cloud Run.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.170
From the project dashboard,
let's go to the product menu

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.470
and select Cloud Run.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.692
Cloud Run home page
shows all services

00:04:20.692 --> 00:04:21.650
running in the project.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.160
When we select our
service, the metrics tab

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.200
will show some of the key
performance indicators

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.290
like request count, broken
MR response code class,

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.190
and latency, with 50th, 95th,
and 99th percentile latencies

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:40.580
automatically calculated.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.830
Metrics that we would want
to use as Service Level

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:48.450
Indicators, or SLIs, are created
for us right out of the box.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.120
We can switch to the logs tab
to see the logs for our service,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.890
and even view them in
the log's Explorer.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.940
Here, we can analyze
and query logs

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.400
from our Cloud Run service
using queries, the field

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.042
Explorer, or the histogram.

00:05:06.042 --> 00:05:07.500
You can review our
previous episode

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.960
on Cloud Logging for
more details if you like.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:14.750
Back on the metrics screen,
we can also open Cloud Trace

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.225
and go directly to the list
of traces for our service.

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.070
We can select a trace and
see detailed information

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.410
for that request,
including total latency

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.200
and any dependencies
our service might have.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.180
This is how container
applications running

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.670
in Cloud Run are
automatically instrumented

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:36.220
to send metrics, logs, and
traces to Cloud Operations.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:41.340
Finally, let's have a look
at how Anthos clusters are

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.950
integrated with Cloud
Operations to provide visibility

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.260
into the infrastructure, no
matter where it's running,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.780
and your services.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:53.240
From the project dashboard,
let's go to GKE clusters.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.000
Here, we have two clusters
running on GKE in the Cloud

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.328
and another cluster
managed by Anthos.

00:05:59.328 --> 00:06:00.870
Let's have a look
at how we can start

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:03.450
to visualize the health of
the cluster and the workloads

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.140
running on it.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.950
Our first dashboard shows us
the status of the control plane,

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.532
including the availability
of the API server, scheduler,

00:06:14.532 --> 00:06:15.490
and controller manager.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.130
We can also see the status
of the infrastructure,

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:25.260
and understand how our
nodes are using CPU, memory,

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.370
and disk resources.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.910
Finally, we can look at the
status of the actual workloads

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:34.750
running in our Anthos cluster,
by understanding metrics

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.330
like container CPU,
memory usage, restarts,

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.720
and network traffic.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.220
To understand more about what's
happening with our container

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.830
workloads, we can go to logging
and look at logs Explorer.

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:50.840
Here, we can look at
Kubernetes container logs

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.850
and filter them through
just the logs being

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:55.475
emitted by services running
in the Anthos-managed cluster.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:02.300
We can scroll down and select
a specific pod or container

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.930
we're actually interested in.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.920
For example, if we want to
get the logs being generated

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:10.070
by our front end, we can select
that as the pod name to filter

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:11.135
the logs to just those.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:15.500
This is how Cloud
Operations can help

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.810
you visualize metrics
and analyze logs being

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.340
emitted by your
container workloads

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.690
running on an Anthos cluster.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.110
Thanks for joining me today.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.027
We covered your options
for running containers

00:07:26.027 --> 00:07:28.780
in Google Cloud, and how to
observe both the infrastructure

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.000
and your services for each one.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.520
I hope this helps you run more
reliable container services,

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.080
and keep your users happy.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:36.303
Thanks so much for watching.

00:07:36.303 --> 00:07:37.720
Don't forget to
Like and Subscribe

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.510
so you never miss out on more
Engineering for Reliability

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:41.500
with Google Cloud.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.380
See you soon.

